  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "adding one only ever affects the last digit, unless that digit is a 9 - then the carry needs to propagate further left",
    "walk the array from the last index toward the first; if the current digit is less than 9, add one to it and you're done immediately",
    "if the current digit is 9, it becomes 0 and the carry continues to the digit before it",
    "what happens if every single digit is 9? the carry runs off the front of the array, and you need one new leading digit (a 1) that wasn't there before"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "converting to a native integer type, which can overflow for the 100-digit constraint",
    "forgetting the case where every digit is 9 and the result needs to grow by one digit at the front",
    "stopping the carry propagation one digit too early or too late",
    "building a new array instead of noticing most digits are unchanged and only the trailing 9s (plus one flipped digit) need to change"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array of digits representing a large non-negative\ninteger, with the most significant digit first and no leading zeroes\n(unless the number itself is a single 0).\n\n**Add one to the integer and return the resulting array of digits, in the same most-significant-first order.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= digits.length <= 100",
      "0 <= digits[i] <= 9",
      "digits does not contain any leading zeroes, except the array [0] itself"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "the array is a single digit",
      "the array is a single 0",
      "adding one causes a carry all the way through, growing the array by one digit (e.g. [9,9,9] -> [1,0,0,0])",
      "no carry occurs at all, only the last digit changes"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
